course: dictionary
lesson: 14869
title: Readily: quick, willing, easy
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Readily is a describing word for how something happens. It can mean quickly and willingly, without fuss. «Mia readily agreed to help tidy the classroom.» Mia did not moan or argue. She said yes straight away and happily.
explain: Readily can also mean easily, without difficulty. «Salt dissolves readily in warm water.» The salt does not need ages of stirring. It mixes in fast and with no trouble. Ask yourself: could I swap in quickly or easily, and does the sentence still make sense?
fact: Readily is an adverb, so it tells you how something is done.
fact: «Readily» means quickly and willingly, without fuss.
fact: «Readily» can also mean easily, without difficulty.
fact: «Readily available» means easy to get or find.
fact: In stories and exams, readily can be a stiffer word for easily or gladly.
exercise: «The shop readily gave a refund when Sam showed the receipt.» What does readily mean here? | *Quickly and willingly, without arguing | Slowly, after a long wait | Angrily | Only just, after a fight
exercise: «Water is readily available from the tap in every school.» What does readily available mean? | Locked away | *Easy to get | Hard to find | Only sometimes hot
exercise: Which word could replace readily in «Sam readily agreed to share his sweets»? | *gladly | slowly | rudely | unwillingly
test: «The children readily accepted the invitation to the party.» Readily here means... | *Quickly and gladly, with no fuss | Sadly, with tears | Angrily, after a long argument | Late, after everyone had gone
test: Which word means the same as readily in «Salt dissolves readily in water»? | *Easily | Slowly | Rarely | Loudly
exam: Which sentence uses readily correctly? | Readily the cat chair under the table. | *The volunteers readily agreed to help with the sports day. | The sky readily sang a loud song. | She readily ate her shoes at lunch.
